 A lollipop  $(C,P)$  {\em is better} than a lollipop   $(C',P')$  if
  \begin{enumerate}
\item[(R1)]  $|V(C)|>|V(C')|$, or %$|V(C)|=|V(C')|$, $(C,P)$ is a nontrivial lollipop and $(C',P')$ is a partial lollipop; or
\item[(R2)] Rule (R1) does not distinguish $(C,P)$  from   $(C',P')$, and $|V(P)-V(C)|>|V(P')-V(C')|$; or
%subject to (a), if a nontrivial lollipop $(C,P)$ exists, then we choose $P$ such that $P$ is as long as possible. Otherwise, if a nontrivial lollipop does not exist, then we choose $(C,P)$ to a be partial lollipop such that $P$ is as long as possible.
