\begin{proof}
Throughout this proof we will use $i_1$ to denote the smallest index such that $u_{i_1} \in S_1 \cup S_2$ and $j_1$ to denote the smallest index such that $u_{j_1} \in S_1$. 

Since $\ell \geq k$, $u_\ell$ has no $H'$-neighbors in $V(C) - V(P)$ by Claim~\ref{allneighbors}. That is, $S_1 =N_{H'}(u_{\ell})$. 
%
%\begin{claim}\label{cXY} Suppose $|S_1 \cup S_2| \geq k$. If some edge $e_j \in E(C)$ intersects $\{u_{i_1+1}, \ldots, u_{\ell}\}$, then $(C,P)$ is a p-lollipop and $e_j = e_c (=f_0)$.
