If both $P_1'$ and $P_2'$  begin with $v_1$,  then some $P_i'$ avoids $f_0$ and first intersects the set $\{u_1, f_1, \ldots, u_{\ell}\}$ at some element $a_j$. Then replacing the segment $v_1, e_c, v_c$ in $C$ with the longer segment $v_1, P_i'[v_1, a_j], a_j, P[a_j, f_0], f_0, v_c$ yields a cycle in $H$ that is longer than $C$, a contradiction. Therefore we may assume that $P_1'$ and $P_2'$ are vertex-disjoint and edge-disjoint in $H$. 
