If some $g_j\in Z$ contains $v_i$ for some $i$ in the $k-2$-element subset $\{2,\ldots,i_1\}\cup \{i_2+1,\ldots,i_3\}$, then in order not to create a longer cycle, the indices of the  remaining $r-1$ vertices of $g_j$ cannot be in $J'_B= \{1,2,\ldots,i_1+1\}\cup \{i_2,\ldots,i_3+1\}$ by Claim~\ref{no2}.
Since $|J'_B|=k+2$, we need $c\geq (r-1)+(k+2)=2k$, a contradiction. So, each $g_j\in Z$ is contained in the set
$F=\{v_{i_1+1},\ldots,v_{i_2}\} \cup \{v_{i_3+1},\ldots,v_c,v_1\}$ which has size $c - (k-2) = k+3 = r+2$. Also, each $g_j$ contains at most one of $v_1,v_{i_2}$ and 
at most one of $v_{i_1+1},v_{i_3+1}$. There are exactly four such possibilities: $F-\{v_1, v_{i_1+1}\},
F-\{v_1, v_{i_3+1}\},F-\{v_{i_2}, v_{i_1+1}\},F-\{v_{i_2}, v_{i_3+1}\}$. So, $z=4$ and each $g_j$ is one of these possibilities, say $g_1=F-\{v_{i_2}, v_{i_1+1}\}$.
But then $g_1$  contains $\{v_c,v_1\}$, and we can replace $e_c$ with $g_1$, and again get a $3$-good dcp-pair. Since $g_1\neq e_c$, this is a contradiction.

 
  {\bf Case 3.2:} $J_B$ is a single interval for each $3$-good lollipop. Let
  $J_B=\{1,\ldots,b\}$ for $b \geq k$. 
