   $r-1=|g_1-v_j|\leq c-(b+1)\leq (2k-1)-(k+1)=k-2$, contradicting $k\leq r$. Hence $g_1\subseteq \{v_{b+1},v_{b+2},\ldots,v_c,v_1\}$, and so $r=|g_1|\leq c-(b-1)\leq (2k-1)-(k-1)=k$. This is possible only it $c=2k-1$, $b=k$, $k=r$ and
   $g_1=\{v_{b+1},v_{b+2},\ldots,v_c,v_1\}$. Let $C'$ be obtained from $C$ by replacing $e_{c-1}$ with $g_1$. Then $(C',P)$ is also a $3$-good dcp-pair. We apply~\eqref{four} to $(C',P)$ to obtain that there exists some $g_1' \in E(H) - E(C')$ contained in $V(C)$
 (possibly $g_1'=e_{c-1}$). Then we contradict Claim~\ref{no2}, since $g_1'\neq g_1$.
   
