Key findings:

- The [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.13190) proves extremal existence, not computational or distributional hardness.
- The theorem-only approach fails G because its contradiction proof begins with a longest cycle and does not construct an executable witness.
- Planting a Berge cycle fixes G and V but fails Track A hardness: a simple incidence walk solved 96/96 tested theorem-valid instances across `n=128,256,512`.
- At `n=512`, the attack needed a median 943 incidence scans and 0.00039 seconds.
- Track B also fails: without exposing the plant there is no paper-backed compact route; exposing it creates an obvious successful in-context attack.

Per the task instructions, no generator, self-test report, README, or oracle transcripts were fabricated after the analytical rejection.
